{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness, specifically whether a lottery is left-skewed — meaning a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable outcomes (higher monetary gains) compared to unfavorable ones (zero or low outcomes). In this case, both lotteries have a zero outcome with high probability (66% and 67%), and a positive outcome (2400€ or 2500€) with the remaining probability. To assess skewness: a lottery is left-skewed if the favorable outcome (positive payoff) has a higher probability than the unfavorable one (zero payoff). However, in both lotteries, the favorable outcome has a lower probability than the unfavorable one (since 34% and 33% are less than 66% and 67%). Therefore, both lotteries are actually right-skewed (more probability on the unfavorable outcome of 0€). Thus, neither lottery is left-skewed. The decision reason, which prefers the more left-skewed lottery, cannot apply to either lottery — both are right-skewed. Hence, the decision reason is not applicable to this decision problem.",
  "step_2": "The verbal report states: 'die wahrscheinlichkeit 0€ zu bekommen ist nur 1% höher. die möglichkeit 100€ mehr zu verdienen lag bei 1% Höher.' This translates to: 'The probability of getting 0€ is only 1% higher. The possibility of earning 100€ more was 1% higher.' This suggests the individual is comparing the difference in probabilities: Lottery B has a 33% chance of 2500€ and 67% chance of 0€, while Lottery A has 34% chance of 2400€ and 66% chance of 0€. The individual notes that the probability of getting 0€ is 1% higher in B (67% vs. 66%), and the probability of earning 100€ more (i.e., 2500€ vs. 2400€) is 1% higher in B (33% vs. 34%). The individual appears to be focusing on the small differences in probabilities and outcomes, suggesting a comparison of expected value or marginal gains. There is no mention of skewness, left-skew, or any evaluation of the distribution shape. The justification is based on small differences in probabilities and outcomes, not on the skewness of the lotteries.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason is about skewness and prefers the more left-skewed lottery. However, in this problem, both lotteries are right-skewed (more probability on the 0€ outcome), so neither is left-skewed. Therefore, the reason cannot be applied. The individual’s report does not mention skewness or distribution shape at all; instead, it focuses on marginal differences in probabilities and outcomes. Since the individual did not consider skewness and the reason is not applicable to the problem, the decision reason was not used. The individual’s reasoning is based on a different criterion (small differences in probabilities and outcomes), not on skewness. Thus, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
